---
.title = "Loam: durable objects",
.description = "How glowcap keeps objects, and the capabilities they hold, across a restart, told through one shared task board.",
.date = .date("2026-10-03T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

Loam is glowcap's storage module for objects and the references they hold.
It runs inside your program, saving their state across restarts and loading
objects as they are used. The stored graph can be larger than memory.

Consider a shared task board. Bob holds a reference to its "to do" column.
Carol keeps a sturdyref to the board, a saved reference that fetches it by
name. Restoring the board means recovering its cards and columns together
with the access those references gave.

Live network references end when the server stops. Peers use saved names to
reconnect to the restored objects.

Programs that host durable objects configure a store during startup. Rooms
already handles that in its session runtime. Peers calling those objects use
ordinary glowcap references and need no local store. The
[integration guide](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam.md#choosing-an-integration)
covers the setup owned by each API.
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers references and sturdyrefs if they
are new to you.

# Objects are records, references are edges

Each object is a **record**: its data, plus **edges** pointing at other
records. "The to-do column holds card #12" is an edge. An edge names a record,
not a memory address, which is why it survives a restart.

A **strong** edge keeps its target alive. A **weak** one doesn't. A **root**
is kept no matter what. Loam keeps anything you can reach from a root along
strong edges.

[Kept: the board is a root and holds two columns strongly, and both columns hold the same card. Reclaimed: a preview that only a weak edge reaches, and an old card and an old column that point at each other but hang from no root.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-loam-graph.svg'))

Everything else is garbage once the running program stops holding it, even two
records that point at each other.
**Collection** deletes it in small batches you schedule.

Changes are **transactions**. Moving a card touches two columns, and a
transaction saves both changes or neither. Saving a transaction is called
**committing** it.

# Objects sleep until called

Most cards on a big board go untouched for months, so Loam lets them
**sleep**: their state stays on disk, and every reference to them keeps
working. A call wakes the card, runs it, saves the result, and only then
replies.

[A call to a sleeping card: the Host accepts it, Loam requests its record, the card's behavior proposes new state, and a storage worker saves it before the reply goes back.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-loam-wake.svg'))

The reply waits for the save so nobody is told about a change a crash could
undo. A crash can still land between the save and the reply, so the change
is kept but the caller never hears back. If the caller retries, the change
can happen twice. Write methods that are safe to repeat, or use managed access
(described below), which keeps receipts for its changes.

Loam's core decides which reads and writes are needed. Its SQLite and LMDB
engines do the disk work, while the surrounding application or binding
schedules it.

[Six layers from top to bottom. Peers call the Host and Vat over CapTP. The Loam attachment wakes actors and saves their state before replying. The Store keeps records, transactions and a bounded cache. A storage worker runs reads and writes, scheduled by the application or binding.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-loam-layers.svg'))

The **attachment** joins a store to the Host that serves your program's
objects to peers.

A card's behavior receives its saved state and a call, then proposes a reply
and new state. It does no I/O. A failed save can discard the proposal, but
cannot unsend an email.

# Restoring access after a restart

To keep Carol's sturdyref working, the board gets a **publication**: a durable
secret name, the swiss number at the end of her sturdyref, saved in an index
on disk.

[Three moments over one store. While the program runs, every change is committed to disk. When it stops, nothing is left in memory, but the store still holds every record and the board's name. After the restart, Carol's fetch wakes the board, her next call wakes the to-do column, and everything else stays asleep on disk.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-loam-restart.svg'))

After a restart nothing loads until somebody asks. Carol's fetch finds the
name and wakes the board alone, and the column she asks for next wakes after
it. Reopen the same store, register its schemas and behaviors, and serve it
under the same reachable peer identity.

References inside a record work the same way. A reference to an object on
another machine can be saved only if you already hold a sturdyref for it.
Loam stores that sturdyref but can't make one for you. A reference that can't be
found again, like an unresolved promise, is refused before anything is
saved.

# Sharing with people

Usually, sharing is handing over a reference. Some applications also need
to see who has access and change it later. **Managed access** keeps that
record for you, beside the objects.

[Alice's board and its columns sit inside one grant's scope. Bob holds the use reference and reads a column. Alice holds the control reference for the same grant and revokes it, and Bob's next read is refused.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-loam-access.svg'))

A **grant** gives permission over a resource and what sits below it. Bob gets
a **use reference**, like a ticket. Alice keeps a separate **control
reference**, like a remote, that can pause, resume, or revoke the grant. Bob
can use his access but not change it.

If Bob shares onward, the new grant sits under his and is never wider. Every
use is checked against all the grants above it, so revoking Bob's grant cuts
off everyone he shared with. Managed access adds no cost to objects that do
not use it.

# [Limits]($section.id('limits'))

- Timers, unresolved promises, subscriptions, and handles to outside
  resources can't be saved. An interrupted call does not resume after restart.
- [Performance]($link.page('performance').ref('persistent-actors-in-loam'))
  charts call latency, memory and restart time from one machine.
- Recovery is tested by killing the process mid-write, not by cutting power.
  The [table of checks](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/stability.md)
  lists every Loam check.
- Loam's full API is in Zig. C and Rust get a narrower subset through
  `libglowcap_loam` and Rust's `loam` feature: create a resource, share it
  with separate controls, edit it and the references it holds, restart, and
  fetch the same durable name again. Compiled for the browser, the same Rust
  feature stores to IndexedDB. TypeScript, Python, and Racket have no direct
  Loam storage API. They can call objects hosted through Loam over CapTP.

[What ships](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/shipped.md#resource-models)
tracks what is built.

# Further reading

Chapter 07,
[durable card](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/examples/zig/07_durable_card.zig),
runs this page's restart as one short program: it moves a card, shuts
everything down, and reopens the store to find the move still there.

1. [Loam guide](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam.md):
   the model, a walkthrough of chapter 07, the words used throughout, and how
   durable actors run.
2. [Stores, transactions and reads](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/store.md):
   commits, the cache, and recovering a write whose outcome is unknown.
3. [Durable names](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/publications.md):
   how a sturdyref finds its actor after a restart.
4. [Durable capability fields](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/references.md):
   saving references to other actors and to objects on other machines.
5. [Collection](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/collection.md):
   deleting unreachable records in small batches while the program runs.
6. [SQLite operations](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/sqlite.md)
   and [LMDB operations](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/lmdb.md):
   sizing, growing, and compacting the store on disk.
7. [Managed access](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/access.md):
   grants, use and control references, revocation, and safe retries.
8. [C and Rust](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/c-and-rust.md)
   and [the browser](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/browser.md):
   the same resources from other languages.
